  "description": "Convert 3-channel color image (H, W, 3) to single-channel 2D grayscale intensity matrix using ITU-R BT.601 perceptual weights.\n\nHuman trichromatic vision possesses maximal retinal sensitivity to green wavelengths, necessitating non-uniform channel weighting in grayscale luminance conversion.\n\n### Mathematical Formulation\n$$\nY = 0.2989 R + 0.5870 G + 0.1140 B\n$$\n\n### Step-by-Step Implementation\n1. Extract red, green, and blue channel intensity matrices\n2. Evaluate weighted sum reflecting human photopic spectral sensitivity\n3. Return 2D grayscale intensity matrix rounded to 4 decimal places\n\n### Returns\nNested 2D list of shape (H, W) representing grayscale pixel values.",
  "theory": "## Understanding the Theory: RGB to Grayscale\n\n### Core Intuition: Why Do We Need This?\nImages have unique spatial properties: neighboring pixels are strongly correlated, and visual patterns (like edges, corners, or textures) appear anywhere in an image. Computer vision algorithms use spatial inductive biases like translation equivariance and local receptive fields to process image grids efficiently. **RGB to Grayscale** operates directly on spatial coordinates or bounding box geometries.\n\nHuman trichromatic vision possesses maximal retinal sensitivity to green wavelengths, necessitating non-uniform channel weighting in grayscale luminance conversion. In this problem, the goal is to: convert 3-channel color image (h, w, 3) to single-channel 2d grayscale intensity matrix using itu-r bt.601 perceptual weights.\n\n### Mathematical Principles & Formulation\nThe core governing equation for this operation is:\n\n$$\nY = 0.2989 R + 0.5870 G + 0.1140 B\n$$\n\nEvery term in this formulation plays a precise mathematical role in ensuring dimensional consistency, numerical stability, and correct algorithmic behavior.\n\n### Step-by-Step Computational Workflow\n1. **Phase 1**: Extract red, green, and blue channel intensity matrices\n2. **Phase 2**: Evaluate weighted sum reflecting human photopic spectral sensitivity\n3. **Phase 3**: Return 2D grayscale intensity matrix rounded to 4 decimal places\n\n### Practical Machine Learning Applications & Nuances\n* **Spatial Locality**: Sliding spatial filters across multi-channel feature maps aggregates contextual clues across local neighborhoods.\n* **Object Detection Pipelines**: Bounding box metrics (such as IoU and Non-Maximum Suppression) reconcile duplicate candidate detections into clean, final predictions.\n* **Data Augmentation**: Geometric transformations expose models to diverse spatial viewpoints, significantly improving generalization to unseen test images.",
  "examples": [
    "Input: image = [[[255, 0, 0], [0, 255, 0]], [[0, 0, 255], [255, 255, 255]]]\n\nOutput: [[76.2195, 149.685], [29.07, 254.9745]]\n\nExplanation: Evaluated cv_rgb_to_grayscale under standard test conditions."
